# Solution — GIAM 6.6 Problem 7

## Problem

If $ S $ and $ T $ are subsets of a set $ D $, what is the product of their characteristic functions $ 1_S \cdot 1_T $?

## Setup

The characteristic function of a subset $ A \subseteq D $ is

$$$
1_A : D \to \{0, 1\}, \qquad 1_A(x) = \begin{cases} 1 & x \in A, \\ 0 & x \notin A. \end{cases}
$$$

The pointwise product $ 1_S \cdot 1_T : D \to \{0, 1\} $ is defined by

$ (1_S \cdot 1_T)(x) \;=\; 1_S(x) \cdot 1_T(x). $

## The Product Is the Indicator of the Intersection

For any $ x \in D $, the product $ 1_S(x) \cdot 1_T(x) $ is $ 1 $ iff both factors are $ 1 $:

$$$
1_S(x) \cdot 1_T(x) \;=\; 1 \;\;\Longleftrightarrow\;\; \bigl(1_S(x) = 1\bigr) \;\text{and}\; \bigl(1_T(x) = 1\bigr) \;\;\Longleftrightarrow\;\; x \in S \;\text{and}\; x \in T \;\;\Longleftrightarrow\;\; x \in S \cap T.
$$$

Otherwise — when at least one factor is $ 0 $ — the product is $ 0 $. So

$$$
\boxed{\, 1_S \cdot 1_T \;=\; 1_{S \cap T}. \,}
$$$

## Verification by Cases

Every $ x \in D $ falls into exactly one of four regions; checking each:

| Where $ x $ lies | $ 1_S(x) $ | $ 1_T(x) $ | $ 1_S(x) \cdot 1_T(x) $ | $ 1_{S \cap T}(x) $ |
| :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: |
| $ S \cap T $              | $ 1 $ | $ 1 $ | $ 1 $ | $ 1 $ |
| $ S \setminus T $         | $ 1 $ | $ 0 $ | $ 0 $ | $ 0 $ |
| $ T \setminus S $         | $ 0 $ | $ 1 $ | $ 0 $ | $ 0 $ |
| $ D \setminus (S \cup T) $ | $ 0 $ | $ 0 $ | $ 0 $ | $ 0 $ |

The fourth and fifth columns agree on every row, so the two functions are equal.

## Remark — Boolean Operations as Pointwise Arithmetic

Characteristic functions translate set-theoretic operations into pointwise arithmetic on $ \{0, 1\} $-valued functions:

- **Intersection.** $ 1_{S \cap T} \;=\; 1_S \cdot 1_T. $ (This problem.)
- **Union.** $ 1_{S \cup T} \;=\; 1_S + 1_T - 1_S \cdot 1_T $ (inclusion–exclusion).
- **Complement.** $ 1_{D \setminus S} \;=\; 1 - 1_S. $
- **Symmetric difference.** $ 1_{S \triangle T} \;=\; 1_S + 1_T - 2\,(1_S \cdot 1_T) $, which mod $ 2 $ is $ 1_S \oplus 1_T $ (XOR).

This is the bridge between set algebra and ordinary arithmetic. Logical "and" on $ x \in S $ and $ x \in T $ becomes multiplication of indicators; "or" becomes inclusion–exclusion; "not" becomes $ 1 - \cdot $. It is the same identification that makes indicator functions central to combinatorics, probability, and measure theory: every Boolean operation on subsets has a clean algebraic counterpart on indicators.
